  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain tomorrow, then drying out for early next \nweek.\n\nRain chances will continue to increase through the overnight\nhours tonight, and the early morning hours on Sunday as a\nsurface low pressure system currently observed in the Big Bend\nof Florida progresses northeastward today and tomorrow. An upper\nlevel trough and associated cold front currently draped across\nthe central Midwest look to be the catalyst to finally drive\nthis surface low up the coast. While this system has been\nassociated with deep subtropical moisture, the 12 UTC sounding\nat Blacksburg analyzed a PWAT of 0.31\". In order for any\nmeaningful rainfall to occur across the region, the dry air over\nthe region needs to be worked over, and the PWAT needs to\nincrease significantly. Overall, most guidance has settled on\nthe region receiving anywhere from 0.10-0.25\" across mountain\nlocations to 0.25-0.66\" east of the Blue Ridge. Some locally\nhigher and lower values are still possible as there is still\nsome variability on both the upper and lower end of the\nspectrum, with the 10% chance of exceedance still around 2.5\ninches across the Piedmont, and around 1.0-1.5\" for areas west\nof the Blue Ridge. Overall, these values are not expected to\nlead to any flooding concerns, but there may be some overflows\nof ditches and drainage areas due to recent leaf debris \nclogging these areas if portions of the region see the higher\nend of the aforementioned values. Behind the passage of the \ncold front on Monday, the area will remain dry through \nWednesday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Precipitation chances increasing for Wednesday \nthrough Thanksgiving.\n\nPrecipitation chances are significantly increasing for\nThanksgiving across the area. By Wednesday, two upper level\nshortwave troughs look to deepen over the central US, and track\neastward. One upper level trough looks to deepen across the Gulf\nstates leading to the development of a surface low along the\nbaroclinic zone off the coast of Florida and Georgia. This upper\nlevel trough looks to possibly become negatively tilted, leading\nto increased divergence aloft over this region. Possible rapid\ncyclogenesis may lead to a rather drenched Thanksgiving for many\nacross the region as this system makes its way up the coastline.\nEventually the upper level trough over the central US looks to\nmerge/phase with the southern stream trough; however, model\nguidance differs wildly at this time at how this may occur. This\nmay increase or decrease rainfall totals depending on how these\nupper level features interact with each other; however, it is \ntoo soon to know at this time. Regardless, confidence is high \nenough in widespread precipitation occurring on Thanksgiving day\nfor the area. Some higher terrain locations above 4000 ft may \ninitially start as a wintry mix thanks to a developing wedge \nearlier in the week; however, warm air aloft is expected to \nquickly transition these areas to all rain. Despite the \nuncertainty in specific timing and extent of impacts, given the \nproximity of this system to major travel days around \nThanksgiving, we encourage the public to stay up to date with \nthe holiday forecast.",
